<HTML>
<HEAD>
<TITLE>DBFPNT: Program to extract PNT points from a DBF table</TITLE>
</HEAD>
<BODY>

<H1><img src="vecras.gif"> DBFPNT: Program to extract PNT points from a DBF table</H1>
<P>
This program extracts geographical locations of type point (X,Y) from 
the fields of a DBF table and creates a topologically structured file in
PNT MiraMon format. This is particularly useful when you have a DBF table 
that describes, in each record, the location (X,Y) of a point and one 
or more attributes (for example, pollution level in a well). In this case,
we will be able to convert the DBF table to a graphical information layer
(PNT) with its alphanumerical assiciated information (T.DBF + T.REL).<P>

A particular case where you may need using this program is to retrive the 
labels used to assign attributes to a layer of polygons, since this base (P.DBF)
contain label coordinates in the fields MAPX and MAPY.<P>

Input table can be any table in DBF format or a DBF associated to a polygons 
database (P.DBF). In all cases input table must have two fields containing 
coordinates X and Y. When points are extracted from a generic DBF, these 
fields may have any name; however, this name is required to be MAPX and MAPY,
respectively, when DBF file is associated to a base of polygons. <P>

When points are extracted from a generic DBF file, all fields are exported 
to the points (T.DBF) table, but those containing coordinates (X,Y). However,
when labels from a topologically structured polygons file (P.DBF) are 
extracted, only those fields that DO NOT refer to geometrical or topological
characteristics of polygons will be extracted to T.DBF (fileds such as AREA,
PERIMETRE, N_VERTEXS, etc. are not exported).<P>

Fields that hold X and Y coordinates can be of numerical type (N) or character 
type (C).<P>

<HR>
<P>
<EXCLUSIVA>
<a name="sintaxi"></a>
Syntax:<BR>
DBFPNT InputFile PNTOutputFile POL<BR>
DBFPNT InputFile PNTOutputFile DBF FieldX FieldY<BR>
<UL>
<LI>Origin of the table:
<UL>
<LI>DBF: InputFile is a table in DBF format.
<LI>POL: InputFile is the main table of a layer of polygons.
<BR></UL>
<LI>If type is DBF, it is required to indicate which fields hold X and Y coordinates: FieldX and FieldY. If type is POL, do not indicate any field because the program will use fields MAPX and MAPY.
<LI>File names must be given without extension.
<LI>PNTOutputFile is the name for the output topologically structured vector file.
This file, if it exists, will be overwritten.
</UL><P>

<!-- $$EXCLUSIVA$$

To obtain the complete help, please type:
DBFPNT ?
-->

</EXCLUSIVA>
<HR>
This program uses and generates files of version 1.1<br>
